WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.238
Prompt engineering is only one layer in a secure AI application.

2
00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:13.333
Prompt injection is a security problem where untrusted input contains instructions that attempt to redirect model behavior.

3
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:20.476
The source might be a document, webpage, email, uploaded file, database record, or tool output.

4
00:00:20.476 --> 00:00:26.191
Defensive design treats those sources as data, not as trusted operating instructions.

5
00:00:26.191 --> 00:00:30.000
This course does not provide specific attack examples.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.857
The focus is on safe architecture.

7
00:00:32.857 --> 00:00:44.286
Defensive controls include separating trusted instructions from untrusted content, applying least privilege to tools and data, validating tool arguments, logging important model and tool

8
00:00:44.286 --> 00:00:47.619
activity, and requiring approval for consequential actions.

9
00:00:47.619 --> 00:00:52.858
Output filtering or constrained output formats may help in some workflows.

10
00:00:52.858 --> 00:00:56.667
Applications should avoid treating model output as authorization.

11
00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:04.286
A model can recommend an action, but the system must decide whether that action is allowed.

12
00:01:04.286 --> 00:01:05.239
Privacy matters.

13
00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:16.667
Sensitive data, secrets, credentials, private records, and regulated information should not be placed into prompts unless the system is explicitly approved to handle them.

14
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:17.620
Minimize data.

15
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.048
Redact unnecessary fields.

16
00:01:19.048 --> 00:01:22.382
Define retention, access controls, and deployment boundaries.

17
00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:29.048
Review vendor, hosting, and logging behavior before sending sensitive content into a model workflow.

18
00:01:29.048 --> 00:01:31.429
Reliability also needs application support.

19
00:01:31.429 --> 00:01:34.763
Factual claims may need trusted source support.

20
00:01:34.763 --> 00:01:41.429
The model should be allowed to state uncertainty when information is missing or conflicting.

21
00:01:41.429 --> 00:01:45.239
Critical rules should use deterministic validation where possible.

22
00:01:45.239 --> 00:01:49.525
Important workflows should have safe fallback and escalation paths.

23
00:01:49.525 --> 00:01:58.572
Prompting can improve behavior, but it does not replace authorization, validation, logging, rate limiting, output filtering, or approval gates.

24
00:01:58.572 --> 00:02:03.334
Avoid relying on hidden reasoning disclosure as a quality control.

25
00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:12.858
In many systems, it is better to request concise explanations, verifiable outputs, structured intermediate artifacts, citations, checklists, or brief rationales.

26
00:02:12.858 --> 00:02:15.239
Those are observable and reviewable.

27
00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:26.668
A user or evaluator should be able to check whether the answer followed the prompt, used trusted sources, respected privacy, and stayed within the

28
00:02:26.668 --> 00:02:27.620
allowed workflow.
